is: True, but not from the premises listed: neither 4.5.1.2 nor 4.5.1.3 says anything about booleans. The sources do, in places no node cites: the Icon book's introduction (icon.txt 504-505, p. xvi) and the Verse paper's section 2.5 (verse.txt 340-341). The node needs 'src: 2.15.4#intro-pxvi 2.15.5#2.5', or those two sentences added to 4.5.1.2 and 4.5.1.3. 'No True and False constructors' is the Verse wording only.
why: Icon: 'Icon, however, uses the concept of the success or failure of a computation, not Boolean values, to drive control structures'. Verse: 'There are no data constructors True and False; instead failure (returning zero values) plays the role of falsity'. The Erlang half follows from 4.5.3.8, and R7 is at report line 157.
by: r3/review-semantic-machinery
at: 2026-11-24

